import { EffectSequencingCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> EffectSequencingCopy = EffectSequencingCopy {
  summary: "An ordinary value type describing whether to continue or stop successfully.",
  taskTitle: "Return a decision to continue or stop",
  purpose: "Use a returned decision when a function finishing one item needs to say whether another item may be processed. The example first creates the two choices, Continue and Break, and prints what each one means.",
  typescriptBefore: "The TypeScript example names two possible string values, continue and break, as LoopControl and describes them with a conditional. In both languages, these are ordinary values whose meaning must be interpreted by their recipient.",
  benefit: "Named callback results distinguish continuing from successful stopping. Returning the decision as a successful value keeps it separate from an expected failure.",
  setup: "Import std/effect under the name effects. effects.LoopControl is the type for the two choices; effects.Continue and effects.Break are its values. Creating either value does not execute anything.",
  after: [
    "fn describe control: effects.LoopControl -> String is an ordinary function taking one control value and returning a description string. match distinguishes the two choices, describing Continue as permission to proceed and Break as successful stopping.",
    "keepGoing and stopHere are names introduced with let. Their : effects.LoopControl annotations state their type. Neither is itself an operation. Like an integer or string, each value can be kept or passed to a function.",
    "effects.succeed stopHere creates an operation whose successful result is that value. The result's type is still LoopControl, now wrapped in something executable. This is the shape needed when a callback, such as the one for forEachUntil, must return an operation.",
    "The do block in effect fn main performs output in order. _ <- println discards the output result. result <- effects.succeed stopHere receives the successful value. result is an ordinary control value and can be passed directly to describe.",
    "This program only prints descriptions; it does not traverse any items. In an actual traversal, forEachUntil waits for each callback operation to complete. Continue allows the next item; Break ends successfully without pulling another item.",
    "forEachUntil succeeds with Unit, a completion-only value, rather than collecting callback results into an array. A failed callback instead stops the traversal with failure. Stopping with Break and stopping because of failure have different outcomes."
  ],
  details: [
    "Putting a plain control value on the right of <- does not make it executable. Where an operation is required, wrap it, for example with effects.succeed effects.Break. To keep an ordinary value, a let binding like stopHere is sufficient.",
    "Break is not an instruction to leave the enclosing do block or an arbitrary loop immediately. A recipient interprets it. In this example, the final println still runs after the Break value has been received.",
    "The two match branches explicitly handle continuing and successful stopping. Failure does not need a third control value: if a callback's operation fails, that failure propagates and later items are not processed."
  ]
}
